Durarara!! AU
Chapter 10 – Part 1
Curtis took the bridge at an easy pace, the red Softail’s engine rolling low under the night air. From the raised vantage he had a clear view of the street below—
—and of the black motorcycle cutting hard through traffic.
Celty.
She was chasing a man in a suit who clutched a briefcase like it contained his entire life. The yellow helmet was locked forward, the black bike closing the distance with predatory precision.
Then a second figure appeared.
A girl in a black hoodie stepped out of an alley as if the shadows had simply decided to stand up. There was no hesitation. A blade flashed under the streetlights. The cut landed clean across Celty’s arm, and the follow-up slash took the yellow helmet clean off her shoulders. It hit the pavement and bounced, rolling into the gutter. The empty space above the black riding suit was suddenly exposed to the open night.
Celty staggered from the impact but didn’t fall.
The girl in the black hoodie was already turning to flee.
Curtis didn’t think.
White smoke erupted from the seams of his jacket and the base of the glowing red helmet, pouring down from the bridge in a dense, controlled mist that swallowed the alley mouth the girl had chosen. He brought the Softail to a sharp stop at street level, killed the engine, and stepped straight into the smoke.
Anri—blade still in hand, Saika’s influence sharp in her movements—spun the moment she sensed someone behind her. The knife came up in a tight, lethal arc meant to open whatever had followed her.
Curtis caught her wrist before the edge could land and pulled her in against him, the white mist swirling thick around both of them.
His voice came through the helmet’s module—cool, filtered, deliberately altered.
“It’s okay. Let’s get out of here.”

Anri’s inner response:
Who—?
The voice isn’t right. It’s filtered. Mechanical. Not a voice I know.
But the grip isn’t trying to hurt me. It’s firm. Controlled. Like he’s more worried about getting me away from the street than about the knife I just tried to put in him.
Celty’s helmet is on the ground. I cut her. The sword is still singing in my head.
And this person in the red-eyed helmet just walked into the mist like he belongs in it and told me it’s okay.
I don’t know who this is.
Why does part of me want to believe him anyway?
Curtis didn’t give Anri time to decide.
White smoke condensed tightly around his own hands and the exposed skin at his neck and wrists, sealing away anything that might have identified him. At the same time a second, lighter mass of the same smoke shaped itself into a simple white helmet that settled over Anri’s head and locked into place with a soft hiss. Her vision narrowed to the tinted interior; the outside world became muted and distant.
He guided her onto the red Softail without ceremony, swung on in front of her, and started the engine in one clean motion. The bike surged forward, red visor cutting through the thinning mist as they left the alley and the bridge behind. Within seconds they were just another pair of lights disappearing into Ikebukuro’s night traffic.
No one on the street had a clear look at either of them.

Celty stood in the middle of the road, the empty space above her shoulders unnervingly exposed under the streetlights. Her yellow helmet lay several meters away in the gutter. The cut on her arm leaked the dark, unnatural fluid that served her instead of blood.
The man with the briefcase was gone.
The girl who had cut her was gone.
There was no trail. No witness pointing the way. No lingering presence she could track. One moment she had been closing in on the possibility of her own head; the next she had been attacked, disarmed of her helmet, and left alone on the asphalt with nothing.
Frustration rolled through her in a rare, sharp wave.
He was right there. The briefcase was right there. It could have been—
She forced the thought down and retrieved her helmet, settling it back into place with controlled movements. The dark visor hid nothing from her, but it hid everything from the city again. Around her, traffic had already begun to flow as if the entire confrontation had been a brief hallucination.
Celty restarted the black motorcycle.
She had lost the trail completely.
And somewhere in the same night, a red Softail with two riders was already several districts away, carrying the one person who had seen everything—and the one person who had done the cutting—into the dark.
Curtis kept the red Softail moving until the bridge and the open street were well behind them. Only when the alleys grew quieter and the neon thinned did he finally slow.
White smoke slipped from beneath the crimson helmet and along his collar. It coiled around the long black braids that had been hanging free, gathering them, compressing them, and tucking the distinctive plaits out of sight against his neck and under the jacket collar. By the time the bike rolled to a stop in a narrow, poorly lit side street, nothing of the braids remained visible.
He killed the engine.
Anri dismounted first, movements still tight and ready. The moment both of her feet were on the ground, Curtis lifted one hand. The white smoke helmet he had formed for her dissolved into thin trails of mist and vanished, leaving her black bob and glasses exposed to the night air again.
The filtered voice came from the glowing red visor, still deliberately altered.
“You’re clear. No one’s following.”
Anri stood in the middle of the alley with the original Saika still humming somewhere under her skin, the residual urge to cut not yet fully quiet. Her eyes—normal brown again, no longer the red of full possession—fixed on the completely sealed figure in the angular black-and-white helmet and gray-and-red jacket.
She didn’t lower her guard.
“…Who are you?” Her voice was quiet, controlled, but edged with the same wary distance she carried at school. One hand remained close to her side, ready to draw the real Saika if the stranger in the glowing helmet proved to be another threat. “Why did you help me?”
The glowing red visor remained fixed on her, unreadable.
Curtis’s filtered voice stayed even and low.
“You weren’t protecting that man when you blocked the Black Rider’s attack. You had a look of disgust at him. But he didn’t see your face—he was too busy running.” A short pause. “As for why I helped… you’re not a bad person. Perhaps you were also tailing him?”
Anri’s fingers twitched once at her side, the presence of Saika still close under her skin. She studied the completely sealed figure in front of her—the angular helmet, the glowing visor, the sharp gray-and-red jacket, the way he stood like someone who had already decided she wasn’t an enemy.
“…You were watching that closely,” she said quietly.
She didn’t confirm or deny the disgust. She didn’t admit to tailing the man with the briefcase either. Instead she held his gaze through the red light and chose her next words with care.
“Most people who interfere in something like that want something. Information. A favor. Or they assume the worst.” Her voice remained soft, but there was a thin edge of caution underneath. “You pulled me out, hid us, and now you’re standing here telling me I’m not a bad person without asking for anything in return.”
A small breath.
“…What do you actually want from me?”
The filtered voice came back without hesitation.
“Not a thing. I don’t want anything.” Curtis shifted his weight slightly, the glowing red visor still steady on her. “Are you going to be okay from here? I don’t even want to know why you did what you did. Just… please promise me you’ll be safe going home from here.”
Anri stared at him.
For a long moment the only sound in the alley was distant traffic. The original Saika had gone quiet under her skin, as if even the blade was listening. No one had ever pulled her out of a situation, refused information, and then asked only for her safety. People always wanted something—protection, answers, control, or the story behind the knife.
This one was asking for a promise and nothing else.
“…You’re serious,” she said softly. It wasn’t quite a question.
She looked down at the pavement for a second, then back up at the sealed helmet.
“I can get home from here. I know these streets.” A small pause. “I’ll be careful.”
It wasn’t a full, warm promise, but it was the most she could give a stranger in a glowing helmet who had already seen too much of what she could do. Her hand finally relaxed away from where Saika waited.
“…Thank you. For not asking.”
Curtis gave a single, short nod.
He said nothing else. The red Softail’s engine rolled back to life, the glowing visor turned away from her, and he pulled out of the alley without looking back. Within seconds the deep growl of the motorcycle faded into the wider noise of Ikebukuro’s night, leaving Anri alone under the weak streetlight.
The quiet only lasted a moment.
Saika’s voice slid through her mind, sweet and insistent, the same endless pressure she had lived with for years.
Why not cut him?
He saved you.
Make him love you.
Anri’s fingers curled slowly at her side.
“Shut up.”
The words were soft, but final. She had said them to the blade more times than she could count. Saika fell into a sulky, circling silence—still present, still watching, but no longer pushing for the moment.
Anri adjusted the strap of her bag, glanced once more in the direction the red motorcycle had gone, and started walking home the long way. The original Saika rested quiet inside her again, and the stranger in the glowing helmet was already gone—asking for nothing, taking nothing, and leaving her with the strange, unfamiliar weight of having been helped without a price.
Curtis went straight home after leaving Anri in the alley.
He parked the red Softail, stored the helmet, and made the rare decision to sleep early. The stitches still pulled. The residual fatigue from blood loss and the long previous day sat heavy in his limbs. If he was going to stay awake through classes, he needed the hours.
He was asleep before midnight.

The next morning at Raira College, the hallways carried the usual post-homeroom traffic.
Anri was walking alone when Takashi Nasujima stepped into her path with that practiced, over-familiar smile. He leaned in just enough to make the space feel smaller, voice low and thick with false concern as he warned her about the recent serial slasher incidents—how a quiet girl like her should be careful, how he was only looking out for his students, how she could always come to him if she felt unsafe.
Anri’s posture tightened the same way it had the last time he cornered her.
She didn’t get the chance to answer.
Curtis Nightstar cut between them without breaking stride, long black braids shifting against the white shirt and blue tie of the school uniform. He didn’t look at Nasujima. He simply reached out, took Anri’s hand in a firm, open grip, and kept walking.
“C’mon, Anri-chan. We’re going to be late for class.” His voice was calm, almost casual, but it carried clearly down the hall. “Can’t do that—we’re the class reps after all.”
He didn’t wait for permission from the teacher or from Anri. He simply guided her past Nasujima, mismatched green and blue eyes flicking once toward the man with a flat, cold assessment that lasted less than a second before he dismissed him entirely.
Nasujima was left standing in the middle of the hallway, the smile frozen and thinning as the two class representatives continued on without looking back.          
They turned the corner, putting solid wall between them and Takashi Nasujima.
Curtis slowed and started to release her hand, the contact having served its purpose.
Anri’s fingers tightened instead.
She didn’t look at him. She simply kept hold, the grip light but deliberate, as if the steady contact was the only thing keeping the leftover tension from Nasujima’s proximity from settling back into her shoulders. Curtis glanced down at their joined hands, then adjusted without comment so the hold was more comfortable than forced. He didn’t mind.
They walked a few more steps in silence before he spoke, voice low enough to stay between them.
“Anri. Are you good?”
Anri kept her eyes forward for a second longer, the familiar soft composure settling back into place one layer at a time. When she answered, her voice was quiet but steady.
“…Yes. Thank you.” A small pause. “He does that sometimes. Pretends it’s concern.”
She still didn’t let go of his hand.
“You didn’t have to step in. But… I’m glad you did.”
Curtis kept his voice even and low as they continued down the quieter stretch of hallway, her hand still resting in his.
“Just stay close if you ever feel uncomfortable. I don’t mind, okay?”
Anri’s steps slowed almost imperceptibly. She glanced sideways at him—at the long black braids, the mismatched eyes, the calm way he offered the same uncomplicated presence he had given her the night before without realizing she would connect the two.
“…Okay,” she answered softly.
The word carried more weight than its size suggested. She didn’t pull her hand away. She didn’t make a larger promise or ask why he was willing. She simply accepted the offer the same way she had accepted the ride out of the alley—quietly, carefully, and with the rare understanding that he meant exactly what he said.
They reached the classroom door still side by side. Only then did Anri’s fingers loosen, though the lingering warmth of the contact remained as she stepped inside ahead of him.
Further down the hallway, half-hidden by the flow of other students, Mikado Ryūgamine had seen the entire thing.
He had noticed Nasujima first—the way the teacher angled his body to block Anri’s path, the too-familiar lean, the smile that never reached his eyes. Mikado’s stomach had dropped the same way it always did when he saw that particular brand of adult attention directed at her. He had already started moving, already half-formed the awkward but determined interruption in his head—
—and then Curtis Nightstar was simply there.
No hesitation. No awkward buildup. He cut between them, took Anri’s hand like it was the most natural thing in the world, and walked her away with a calm “we’re the class reps” that left Nasujima standing uselessly in the middle of the hall.
Mikado stopped where he was, one hand still half-raised.
Again.
He did it again.
First the class representative vote while I was still deciding whether to raise my hand.
Then the street with those girls and the guy in the yellow hoodie.
Now this.
Every time I see something happening to Anri and start to move, he’s already handled it. Like the window for me to do anything useful closes the second he appears.
He watched the two of them disappear around the corner, Anri still holding Curtis’s hand even after they were out of Nasujima’s sight. Something tight and complicated settled under Mikado’s ribs—relief that she was okay, frustration that he hadn’t been the one to step in, and a quieter, harder-to-name feeling he didn’t want to examine too closely in the middle of the hallway.
Who even is this guy?
Mikado lowered his hand, adjusted the strap of his bag, and started walking toward class with the uncomfortable certainty that Curtis Nightstar was becoming a fixed point in the spaces Mikado kept meaning to occupy.